# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials — nothing here may ever be committed.
#
# Authentication uses Application Default Credentials, so there should be no key
# file in the working tree at all. These patterns exist for the day one appears
# anyway: a downloaded key, a script that wrote one, a copy made "just to test".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
google-cloud-key.json
service-account.json
gcp-service-account-key.json
*-service-account*.json
*serviceaccount*.json
application_default_credentials.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment variables
#
# ⚠️ READ THIS BEFORE ADDING `.env.production` BELOW.
#
# `frontend/.env.production` IS TRACKED, DELIBERATELY, and must stay tracked.
# Next.js compiles NEXT_PUBLIC_* values into the browser bundle at BUILD time,
# and the build happens inside the container (see frontend/Dockerfile). If that
# file is not in the repository, the image builds cleanly with every value
# empty — producing a site that deploys successfully and cannot sign anyone in.
#
# Its contents are public by design: they identify the Firebase project, they do
# not grant access to it. Access is decided by Firebase Auth, by the Firestore
# rules that deny browsers entirely (Decision 004), and by the browser key's
# referrer restriction. That last one is not decoration — on 2026-09-01 it was
# found to be the thing that had silently broken password recovery, which is how
# we learned how load-bearing it is.
#
# The `.example` files are also tracked, on purpose: they are the documentation
# of which variables exist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local tooling settings
#
# `.claude/settings.local.json` holds machine-specific permission grants. Until
# 2026-09-01 it was ignored only by this machine's global git config, which
# meant a clone on any other computer would have started tracking it. The rest
# of `.claude/` is framework-owned and IS tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/settings.local.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependencies and build output
#
# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins the exact version of
# every library, so the code that runs in production is the code that was
# tested. Ignoring it lets two builds of the same commit differ.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml

.next/
out/
dist/
build/
.vercel/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs, caches, scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
npm-debug.log
yarn-error.log
pnpm-debug.log
firebase-debug.log
firestore-debug.log
.cache/
coverage/
*.tmp
temp/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and operating systems
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
